Reduction to a few combinators is old in combinatory logic. The device special to this model is (3.1), which folds the arithmetical combinators into the one combinator $\mathbf{G}$. The Gödel numbering and the second recursion theorem are the standard constructions, written so that arithmetic and the type-free combinators share a notation, and the incompleteness theorem is the standard one in that notation. Theorem 3.5 is the argument of [@Myhill55]; Martin Hyland pointed out the connection. Theorem 3.6 restates enumeration degrees, as in [@Rogers67], [@Sasso75], and [@Gutteridge71]. Scott notes that this area was underdeveloped beside the theory of Turing degrees. Semigroups of combinators had been discussed by Church and by Böhm, and Theorem 3.7 reads that discussion inside $\mathbf{RE} \cap \mathbf{FUN}$.

A great advantage of the combinators from the formal point of view is that (bound) variables are eliminated in favor of “algebraic” combinations. The disadvantage is that the algebra is not all that pretty, as the combinations tend to get rather long and general laws are rather few. Nevertheless as a technical device it is mildly remarkable that we can have a notation for all r.e. sets requiring so few primitives. In the model defined here the reduction to one combinator rests on a lemma about conditionals:

$$(3.1) \quad \mathbf{cond}(x)(y)(\mathbf{cond}(x)(y)) = y.$$

Recall that $\mathbf{cond}$ (or $\supset$) is a test for zero, so that:

$$(3.2) \quad \mathbf{cond}(x)(y)(0) = x.$$

This suggests that we lump all combinators of Theorem 2.4 into this one:

$$(3.3) \quad \mathbf{G} = \mathbf{cond}(\langle \mathbf{suc}, \mathbf{pred}, \mathbf{cond}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{S} \rangle)(0).$$

We can then readily prove Theorem 3.1 below.
